nk: Count', e.g., '2: 4' means there are four 2s left unknown)\nA: 1, J: 1, 10: 1, 9: 1, 5: 1\nValid Moves:\nsingle: ♥6\nsingle: ♥10\n\nSelect the best move from the Valid Moves list above. You can choose to pass even if you have valid moves that can beat the previous play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': "**Assessing the Optimal Play**\n\nI've been meticulously analyzing the board, carefully considering card distributions and potential outcomes. Focusing on strategic blocking, I've concluded that playing the ♥10 is the superior move, primarily to force a higher response from my teammate and prevent a potentially disastrous situation. I've considered all the possible card distributions.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: The Landlord has only 1 card remaining and passed on a King, meaning they hold a card lower than King (J, 10, 9, or 5). Teammate acts before the Landlord. Playing the ♥6 is risky because if the Teammate plays a low card (like a 9 or 10) to save their high cards, the Landlord might beat it with a 10 or J and win the game immediately.\n\nReason: By playing the ♥10, I force my Teammate to play a higher card (Jack or Ace) to beat it. Since the Landlord passed on the King, they cannot beat a Jack or Ace. This effectively blocks the Landlord from winning on this turn, regardless of whether they hold a 10, 9, or 5. Playing the 10 eliminates the risk of the Teammate underplaying and handing the win to the Landlord.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♥10"]}'}
